.TH AUDIO 4 "June 2026" "Substrate Kernel" "Kernel Interfaces"
.SH NAME
audio, audioctl \- Substrate audio device interface
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <sys/audioio.h>
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B audio
framework provides a Sun/NetBSD-compatible PCM playback and capture
interface.  Each registered audio device is exposed as a pair of device
nodes under
.BR /dev/ ,
backed by a hardware-specific driver that plugs an ops vector into the
common framework.
.PP
The framework owns parameter validation, the shared
.BR ioctl (2)
dispatcher, exclusive-access arbitration, and
.BR devfs
publication; the backends own only the data path.
.SS Device Nodes
.TP
.B /dev/audioN
The sample device for the
.IR N -th
audio device.  Bytes
.BR write (2)\c
\&-ten here are queued for playback;
.BR read (2)
returns captured samples on devices that support recording.
.TP
.B /dev/audioctlN
The control device.  It accepts the same
.BR ioctl (2)
requests as
.BR /dev/audioN
but may be opened while the sample device is busy, so a mixer or status
tool can inspect and adjust a stream another process is playing.
.TP
.B /dev/audio
A convenience alias for
.BR /dev/audio0 ,
registered when the first audio device appears.  Most userland
(and NetBSD-personality binaries) open
.B /dev/audio
without a unit suffix.
.SS Sample Format
The default and native framework format is signed 16-bit linear
.RB ( AUDIO_ENCODING_SLINEAR_LE ),
stereo.  Sample rate, channel count, precision, and encoding are
negotiated per device with
.BR AUDIO_SETINFO ;
a backend may clamp the request to what the hardware supports and report
the effective values back through
.BR AUDIO_GETINFO .
.SS Exclusive Access
Playback is exclusive.  The per-device software FIFO and DMA path are
single-producer; if two processes wrote concurrently the FIFO would
corrupt and both would wedge.  The first process to
.BR write (2)
claims the device; a second process's
.BR write (2)
fails with
.BR EBUSY .
The claim is released when the owning process closes the device or
exits.  A blocked
.BR write (2)
(FIFO full, waiting on the hardware to drain) is interruptible: a
delivered signal breaks the wait, so a player can always be
interrupted or killed.
.SS Backends
The following drivers register with the framework when their hardware is
present:
.RS
.IP \(bu 4
.B hda
\(em Intel High Definition Audio.
.IP \(bu 4
.B ac97
\(em AC'97 codecs.
.IP \(bu 4
.B sb16
\(em Sound Blaster 16 (ISA).
.IP \(bu 4
.BR uac (4)
\(em USB Audio Class headsets and DACs.
.IP \(bu 4
.B null
\(em a bit-bucket backend for hardware-less testing (see
.B KERNEL OPTIONS
below).
.RE
.SH IOCTLS
.TP
.B AUDIO_GETINFO
Retrieve the current
.B audio_info_t
(play and record parameters plus runtime statistics).
.TP
.B AUDIO_SETINFO
Set play and/or record parameters.  Fields left at their initialized
"no change" value
.RB ( AUDIO_INITINFO )
are preserved.
.TP
.B AUDIO_DRAIN
Block until all queued playback samples have been emitted.
.TP
.B AUDIO_FLUSH
Discard queued playback and capture samples immediately.
.TP
.B AUDIO_GETDEV
Retrieve an
.B audio_device_t
identifying the backing hardware (name, version, config).
.SH KERNEL OPTIONS
.TP
.B audio_null
Register the
.B null
backend.  It is
.I off
by default: as a first-come device it would claim
.B /dev/audio0
and shadow a real device that registers later (notably
.BR uac (4),
which is enumerated after the built-in controllers), sending
.B /dev/audio
to the bit bucket.  Pass
.B audio_null
on the kernel command line to exercise the audio stack with no
hardware present.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /dev/audio
Default sample device (alias for
.BR /dev/audio0 ).
.TP
.B /dev/audioN
Sample devices.
.TP
.B /dev/audioctlN
Control devices.
.SH ERRORS
A
.BR write (2)
to a sample device may fail with:
.TP
.B EBUSY
Another process already holds the device for playback.
.TP
.B EINTR
A signal was delivered while the write blocked waiting for the hardware
to drain, before any data was queued.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ioctl (2),
.BR read (2),
.BR write (2),
.BR uac (4)
.SH HISTORY
The
.B audio
framework follows the Sun/NetBSD
.B audioio
interface.  Exclusive-access arbitration and interruptible playback
waits were added to prevent concurrent-use hangs.
